WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.700
This video is sponsored by Dashlane.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.930
Conveniently store your passwords and get
10% off with the link in the description.

00:00:07.930 --> 00:00:14.690
In 1997, a guy named Jeffrey Bezos filed US
patent 5960411A,

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:18.490
“a method for ordering items over the internet”.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:23.570
Here’s what it is: Instead of adding an
item to your shopping cart and then clicking

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:25.929
buy, you’d just click buy.

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:28.010
That’s the whole patent.

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:33.910
It’s so comically vague because that’s
what makes intellectual property so powerful.

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:37.850
The wider the description, the easier it is
to enforce.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:43.320
Just listen to this one: “An apparatus for
use as a toy by an animal, for example, a

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.660
dog, to either fetch, carry, or chew.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:50.610
Includes a main section with at least one
protrusion extending therefrom that resembles

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:51.610
a branch.”

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.410
That, my friend, is a stick.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:01:00.170
Both of these are ridiculously broad, but,
unfortunately, that’s not uncommon.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:05.380
What is unique about Amazon’s One-Click
Patent is that they actually use it.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:10.390
Usually these are filed by Non-Practicing
Entities, companies that own intellectual

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:13.250
property, but don’t do anything with it.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:17.250
Instead, they make money by suing anyone and
everyone.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.330
These two graphs tell the story.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:25.140
First, this is the amount of patent-related
litigation from normal, practicing companies.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:28.770
Year after year, the number stays pretty much
the same.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.770
And here are the trolls.

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:36.760
Not only are they way more common, but they’ve
rapidly increased in just a few years time.

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:42.250
And second, roughly 87% of troll lawsuits
settle before trial.

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:47.910
In other words, patent trolls are very common
and very good at what they do.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:53.900
Their strategy is fairly simple: File or buy
as many and as broad of patents as possible,

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.760
and then find people to sue.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:02.190
Both they and the other company know the cost
of going to trial - hundreds of thousands,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:04.630
or usually, millions of dollars.

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:09.290
So, unless both parties are really confident,
they make a deal.

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:15.170
The company pays the troll just to leave them
alone, who, by the way, now has even more

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.730
resources to threaten even more lawsuits.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.460
You don’t need much intellectual property
to see how this is a broken system.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:27.660
But the problem is actually much worse than
that.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:34.950
Okay, so, you’ve got the next big idea,
a zero-click, mind-reading buy now button.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.800
Here’s what you need to know about patents.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.920
To get one, your invention must be novel,
useful, and non-obvious,

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.560
ya know, like a buy now button which buys…
now.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.450
Totally not obvious.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.450
Anyway,

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:54.950
It also can’t be a law of nature, an abstract
idea, or a nuclear bomb.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:55.950
Sorry!

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.550
I don’t write the rules.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:03.890
They usually last 20 years, and unlike copyright,
patents are not automatic.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.060
In the United States, you must apply to the
Patent and Trademark Office.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:12.880
And so long as your application has a left
margin of two and a half centimeters and its

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:20.690
capital letters are at least 0.3175 centimeters
high, it’ll be reviewed by an examiner…

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:23.200
with all the speed of the DMV.

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.030
The process takes about 2 years,

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:31.230
but the office does provide patent-themed
Halloween and Valentines Day entertainment

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.090
while you’re waiting.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:34.090
Seriously.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:38.760
Once your patent is granted, it prevents anyone
else from making the same thing without a

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.760
license.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.620
In other words, it’s a two-decade, government-protected
monopoly.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.750
So, why do they exist?

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.840
The answer you’ve probably heard is pharmaceuticals.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.030
Researching and developing a new drug, is,
well, gosh darn expensive.

00:03:55.030 --> 00:04:00.500
Some say 648 million dollars, others, 2.7
billion.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.970
Whatever the number, it’s a lot.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.970
Without patents, they say, no company would
spend so much when they could just wait for

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:10.250
someone else to, and then steal their idea.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:15.490
Someone could jump in and take all the profit
from the person actually doing the work.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:16.960
Sound familiar, maybe?

00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:19.209
So, patents are an incentive.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:25.200
And not just to make things you and I want,
but also to explain how they work.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.710
Because sharing knowledge is kind of the basis
for civilization.

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:33.870
This way, when it expires, others can make
it at a lower cost.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.410
Or, that’s the theory.

00:04:35.410 --> 00:04:39.480
The issue is, patents aren’t always good
explanations.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.930
In fact, that’s by design.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:46.120
Because, remember, companies keep them as
vague as possible.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:50.200
Technically patents should be so specific
that a person with “ordinary skill in the

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:57.160
art” could reproduce it, but technically
they should also be non-obvious, soo… yeah.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.710
The other issue is that patents aren’t the
only option.

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:07.560
Google’s search algorithm, WD-40, and Coca-Cola
all famously chose to just keep their mouths

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:08.560
shut.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:14.699
Trade secrets do run the risk of being discovered,
but they don’t expire in 20 years and they’re

00:05:14.699 --> 00:05:16.320
still legally protected.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.480
So a smart company asks itself: Can we keep
the secret for 20 years?

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:23.500
If not, it should get a patent.

00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.880
But otherwise, it’d be better off staying
quiet.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:32.320
Because of this, patents may not disclose
anything that wouldn’t already be discovered.

00:05:32.320 --> 00:05:36.730
What isn’t really in question is that they
can be a good incentive.

00:05:36.730 --> 00:05:41.980
Of course companies are more likely to create
something if it grants them a monopoly.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.710
The question is whether it’s worth it.

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.770
Because during that monopoly, we all pay a
price - the higher cost they can charge with

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:51.340
no competition.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.930
When your phone costs $100 more, this is annoying.

00:05:55.930 --> 00:06:00.320
When the World Health Organization estimates
ten million lives could’ve been saved with

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.800
existing medicines and vaccines, it’s downright
awful.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:07.010
And then there’s the problem of waste.

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.720
Patents, like any economic system, are a game.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:16.890
And when you add seven billion players, there
are going to be some unintended consequences.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.270
The obvious one are the trolls.

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:24.010
Their defense is that those who invent something
may not be the best people to sell it.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:25.440
Universities, for example.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.310
Lots of research, but Marketing, not so much.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:34.070
So, they say, Non-Practicing Entities buy
these inventions, from, say, a school, and

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.240
sell them to those who can use it.

00:06:36.240 --> 00:06:39.400
But ask those buyers and… they shake their
heads.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:45.380
In this survey, 92% of respondents said they
added new products or features from the license

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.669
zero to ten percent of the time.

00:06:47.669 --> 00:06:51.930
And they “rarely, if ever prompted the development
of any new products”.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:56.980
In other words, companies had already invented
something and bought the license only to not

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:57.980
get sued.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:03.770
Microsoft, for example, takes or took money
from HTC, Samsung, and Apple for patents like

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:06.199
scheduling meetings on smartphones.

00:07:06.199 --> 00:07:11.870
Its phone was, to put it politely, a failure,
but it still collects profit, and on a feature

00:07:11.870 --> 00:07:14.030
that nobody hardly uses.

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:18.090
You might be thinking “So what?”, Who
cares that the world’s richest companies

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:19.720
are losing some money?

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:24.770
But all these billions of dollars spent on
lawsuits is money not spent developing new

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.350
products or hiring employees.

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:32.930
When Google bought Motorola, largely for its
patents, it spent 12 and a half billion dollars

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:34.780
instead of who knows what.

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:37.540
This is what I call Patent Theater,

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:42.460
In the same way that so much of airport security
is just acting out the motions for little

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:43.750
or no benefit,

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:48.870
Big companies often file and buy patents not
because they actually need them, but because

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:51.120
that’s the game they need to play.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.150
It’s not about innovation, it’s about
not getting sued.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:59.580
Chances are any one phone company will violate
another’s patents.

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:03.000
But file enough of its own, and it can threaten
a countersuit.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.680
It’s mutually assured destruction.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:11.670
It might be better if no-one had them, but
someone does, so everyone has to.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:16.630
While researching this video, I came across
this article titled “The world’s 50 most

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:18.130
innovative companies”.

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.130
It’s just a list of who has the most patents.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:26.419
Of course, number of patents doesn’t really
measure innovation, it measures the, well…

00:08:26.419 --> 00:08:27.970
number of patents.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:29.710
That probably sounds obvious.

00:08:29.710 --> 00:08:31.350
But, it shouldn’t be.

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:34.639
Their purpose is precisely to increase innovation.

00:08:34.639 --> 00:08:36.860
This should be an accurate list.

00:08:36.860 --> 00:08:39.750
It’s not because of all the unnecessary
waste.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.779
Worse, there isn’t really anyone to blame.

00:08:42.779 --> 00:08:49.119
Examiners have to review half a million applications
a year and under pretty stressful circumstances.

00:08:49.119 --> 00:08:50.860
Mistakes will be made.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:54.829
And companies have to file patents or risk
being targeted.

00:08:54.829 --> 00:08:59.420
There is a solution to all of this, but that’s
the topic of the next video.

